(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-2-cardiovascular-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Advanced Cardiovascular Examination Quiz 1 — PD2 Exam 2","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does a hyperkinetic ventricular impulse indicate?","choices":["Chronic pressure load with ventricular hypertrophy","Chronic volume overload with ventricular dilation","Pericardial inflammation","Transiently increased stroke volume"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it does not necessarily indicate heart disease — anxiety, hyperthyroidism and severe anaemia are the examples. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What distinguishes tricuspid from mitral stenosis on auscultation?","choices":["Its timing in systole","Its radiation to the axilla","Its location near the xiphoid","Its high pitch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both are mid to late diastolic low-pitched rumbles of grade 1 to 4 heard with the bell, so location is what separates them. Tricuspid stenosis follows an opening snap. That is the lower left sternal border. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is a bruit?","choices":["A high-pitched sound from a stenotic mitral valve opening","A murmur-like sound of vascular origin","A palpable vibration from a loud murmur","A scratchy sound from pericardial inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, auscultated over the aorta, renal, iliac and femoral arteries. Not of cardiac origin. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which sounds is the diaphragm better for?","choices":["Bruits over peripheral arteries only","S1, S2 and rubs","S3, S4 and mitral stenosis","Only sounds at the apex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the high-pitched sounds. It is pressed firmly and used to listen throughout the precordium. The regurgitant murmurs too. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What defines a pansystolic murmur?","choices":["It begins after a click in mid systole","It spans both systole and diastole","It begins with S1 and continues to S2","It occupies the middle of systole only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — something is open through all of systole, with blood flowing from a high pressure chamber to a lower one through a structure that should be closed. All are pathologic. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"How is dyspnoea defined?","choices":["An unpleasant awareness of the heartbeat","Awareness of breathing out of proportion to exertion","Breathlessness that occurs only when supine","Sudden breathlessness waking the patient from sleep"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the qualifier matters: it is the mismatch with the level of exertion rather than breathlessness as such. It is common in both cardiac and pulmonary problems. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which component of S2 is usually louder, and why?","choices":["Aortic, reflecting the high pressure in the aorta","Pulmonic, because the valve is closer to the chest wall","They are always equal in intensity","Whichever closes second"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A2 and P2 are the two components, and the pressure difference between the two circuits is what makes the aortic one dominate. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What causes an opening snap?","choices":["Rapid ventricular filling against a compliant ventricle","Abrupt deceleration as a stenotic mitral valve opens","Closure of the aortic and pulmonic valves","Ballooning of the mitral valve into the left atrium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, producing a very early diastolic sound. As the leaflets calcify over time it becomes less audible. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How long is the patient asked to clench the fist in the Allen test?","choices":["Two seconds","Ten minutes","Until the patient reports pain","Thirty seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with both arteries occluded. The fist is then opened and one artery released to watch the hand fill. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"What characterises an innocent midsystolic murmur?","choices":["A structural abnormality of the heart or great vessels","Always grade 4 or above with a thrill","No physiologic or structural abnormality","A physiologic change in body metabolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, usually from increased blood flow. It diminishes when the patient stands, sits up, or performs a Valsalva, and is common in infancy and childhood, disappearing by adulthood. It grades 1 to 3 out of 6. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What does a sustained ventricular impulse indicate?","choices":["Ventricular dilation from increased preload","Inflammation of the pericardial sac","Hypertrophy from chronic pressure load","Transiently increased stroke volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — increased afterload. Aortic stenosis and hypertension are the examples, and the duration is sustained rather than under two thirds of systole. That is increased afterload. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the more common mechanism of S4?","choices":["Turbulent flow across an incompetent valve","Hypertrophy or fibrosis stiffening the ventricle","High filling pressures with abrupt deceleration of inflow","Sudden opening of a stenotic mitral valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the stiff ventricle resists filling following atrial contraction. Hypertensive heart disease, aortic stenosis, ischaemic and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and delayed atrioventricular conduction are the causes. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the most common symptom of coronary artery disease?","choices":["Chest pain","Palpitations","Syncope","Peripheral oedema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the differential to hold alongside it is angina pectoris, myocardial infarction, dissecting aortic aneurysm and pulmonary embolism. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is true about the popliteal pulse?","choices":["It is the easiest lower limb pulse to find","Its absence always indicates arterial disease","It is palpated with the knee fully extended","It is harder to feel, and sometimes absent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and this may not matter if the more distal pulses are present. With the knee flexed, press fingertips deeply into the popliteal fossa. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What are varicosities?","choices":["Mild to severe dilatation of veins","Thickened arterial walls with reduced elasticity","Brown discolouration from haemosiderin","Ulceration around the medial malleolus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they are among the associated findings of chronic venous insufficiency along with oedema, purpura, stasis dermatitis and dependent cyanosis. Mainly in the lower extremities. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which lighting is specified for the cardiovascular examination?","choices":["Bright overhead lighting","Dim lighting to reduce patient anxiety","Coloured light to highlight cyanosis","Tangential lighting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because it casts shadows that reveal the contours of the chest wall — a pulsation visible in raking light can be invisible under direct light. It matters most during inspection. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How can palpating the carotid help with murmur timing?","choices":["The carotid pulse identifies the murmur's intensity","The carotid pulse indicates the shape of the murmur","Systolic murmurs match the carotid upstroke","Diastolic murmurs coincide with the carotid upstroke"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a systolic murmur falls between S1 and S2, a diastolic one between S2 and S1, and the carotid gives you an external marker for which is which. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How is pitting oedema tested for?","choices":["Press on the nail bed and time the refill","Dorsiflex the foot forcefully with the knee bent","Press firmly with the thumb, two seconds","Press with the balls of the hand against the limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, over the dorsum of the foot, posterior to the medial malleolus, and over the shins. The pit is the depression the thumb leaves. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which states produce a physiologic midsystolic murmur?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and atrial septal defect","Mitral and tricuspid regurgitation","Anaemia, pregnancy, fever, thyrotoxicosis","Aortic stenosis and pulmonic stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — changes in body metabolism rather than a structural abnormality, which is what separates them from the pathologic group. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is required of stethoscope placement?","choices":["Placement over a thin layer of clothing is acceptable","Contact anywhere on the chest wall is equivalent","Firm pressure in every case","Direct contact with the patient's skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is also why the examination should never be done over the patient's clothing and why the patient should be in a gown. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How should the patient be prepared before brachial pressure is measured for an ankle brachial index?","choices":["Supine and resting for ten minutes","Seated with feet flat for five minutes","Standing for two minutes","Immediately on arrival, with no rest"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the cuff is inflated 20 mm Hg above the last audible pulse then deflated at about 1 mm Hg per second, with two measurements averaged in each arm. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What does the Allen test evaluate?","choices":["Patency of the radial and ulnar arteries","Presence of deep vein thrombosis","The ratio of ankle to brachial pressure","The adequacy of capillary refill"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — compress both, have the patient clench for thirty seconds, then open the fist and release one artery, watching the hand fill. They supply the hand. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"How is an opening snap distinguished from the pulmonic component of S2?","choices":["By its high pitch and obvious snapping quality","By its low pitch and rumbling quality","By its variation with the respiratory cycle","By being heard only with the bell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which matters because a loud snap can radiate to the apex and to the pulmonic area and be mistaken for P2. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What happens when a patient stands quickly from squatting?","choices":["Blood pools in the legs; preload falls","Blood returns from the legs and preload rises","Intrathoracic pressure rises","Afterload rises through muscular contraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — less blood returns to the heart, so the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy murmur becomes louder and the aortic stenosis murmur softer. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How is the murmur of mitral stenosis best heard?","choices":["With the diaphragm at the base, sitting forward","With the diaphragm at the apex, supine","With the bell in the aortic area","Bell at the apex, left lateral decubitus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a low-pitched decrescendo rumble, mid to late diastolic, grade 1 to 4, with little or no radiation. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which conditions cause a pathologic S3?","choices":["Pericarditis","Decreased contractility, heart failure, shunts","Hypertensive heart disease and aortic stenosis","Mitral valve prolapse"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with the volume overload coming from aortic or mitral regurgitation — all conditions that raise left ventricular filling pressure. Ventricular volume overload as well. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What are the skin findings of chronic arterial insufficiency?","choices":["Thin shiny skin, hair loss, cool to touch","Thickened skin with brown pigmentation and pitting oedema","Normal temperature with cyanosis when dependent","Warm erythematous skin with bounding pulses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with no oedema, painful ulceration unless neuropathy is present, gangrene, decreased pulses, foot pallor on elevation and dusky rubor. The colour is pale or dusky red. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"What can the murmur of aortic regurgitation be mistaken for?","choices":["S3","Breath sounds","A pericardial friction rub","An opening snap"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because it is high frequency and blowing. It is early diastolic, decrescendo, grade 1 to 3, from the aortic area down the left intercostal space. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What produces a murmur?","choices":["Closure of the atrioventricular valves","Inflammation of the pericardial sac","Abrupt deceleration of a stenotic valve opening","Turbulent blood flow over a valve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it sounds like a swoosh. Five situations produce it: obstructed valve, increased flow through a normal valve, ejection into a dilated chamber, regurgitant flow, and abnormal shunting. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In whom is S4 considered normal?","choices":["Nobody — S4 is always pathologic","Trained athletes and older populations","Children and young adults","Women in the last trimester of pregnancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is the mirror of S3 — physiologic in the young and pathologic in the old, while S4 runs the other way. — PD II Advanced Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular System - Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
